{"qid":"Q1761381","names":["Giudicato of Logudoro"],"title":"Giudicato of Logudoro","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Giudicato of Logudoro (Sardinian: Judicadu de Logudoro; also called the Giudicato of Torres after its capital) was one of the four autonomous kingdoms (giudicati) that governed Sardinia following the collapse of Byzantine administration in the ninth century. Occupying the northwestern quarter of the island, it was the largest of the four giudicati and maintained important commercial ties with Genoa and Pisa, whose merchants and clergy played growing roles in Logudorese affairs from the eleventh century onward. The giudicato was weakened by internal dynastic conflicts and Genoese encroachment; the last ruler, Queen Adelasia, died without a direct heir around 1259, and the territory was partitioned between the Genoese Doria and Malaspina families rather than passing intact to a successor state.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The general territorial extent of the giudicato is known from medieval Sardinian administrative records and Italian mercantile documents, but precise internal boundaries between the four giudicati are reconstructed approximately and disputed in scholarship.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Judicate of Logudoro","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judicate_of_Logudoro"},{"label":"Britannica: Sardinia history","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Sardinia/History"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Judicate of Logudoro","rulers":[{"qid":"Q2279633","name":"Gonario I of Lacon Gunale","start":1015,"end":1038,"image":null},{"qid":"Q2279550","name